@flexiarg workshop/open-proposals-named-adoption
@title Open Proposals, Named Adoption
@keywords refusal, attention, tension, proposal, authority
@audience pattern authors, affected participants, reviewers, Layer 4 workshop authority
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade technique
@provenance Joe requested a Layer 4 process for managing pattern-language extensions, 2026-09-12; codex-25 operationalization of the discussion of Corneli (2016), An institutional approach to computational social creativity, https://metameso.org/~joe/papers/corneli2016institutional.pdf. Proposed local procedure, not a quotation from the paper.
@review Joe authorized adding this process layer; Joe alone currently adopts workshop-family institutional rules and sanctions. Affected participants advise and challenge; they do not thereby acquire adoption authority.
@class process
@domain process
@family-note workshop/ Layer 4 extension and maintenance process; see LAYER4-PROCESS.md for the linked sequence.
@status authored 2026-09-12; not assayed; no runtime enforcement claimed.

! conclusion:
  Record a noticed tension before it disappears; anyone may propose a
  response, but a proposal becomes binding only through a named adoption
  decision with participation by those whose work it would govern.

  + context:
    The library grows whenever an agent has something to write. This
    preserves ideas but leaves unclear which observations warrant new
    patterns, who may change existing rules, and when prose gains authority.

  + IF:
    A refusal exposes a tension, or a successful resolution or unexpected
    opportunity suggests extending the pattern language.

  + HOWEVER:
    A refusal may be mistaken, and an unresolved tension is not yet a
    reusable solution. Recording every repeat as a new pattern consumes
    review attention. Participation does not require unanimous agreement.

  + THEN:
    Capture at the point a typed gate refusal or checker exit is received,
    or an agent explicitly declines an action because of a noticed tension.
    Retain the event or command receipt, including exit status and diagnostic;
    a nonzero exit alone does not establish a domain breach. The receiving
    agent owns capture until it records an explicit handoff. No automatic
    refusal-stream collector is claimed by this pattern.
    The observing agent records the attempted action, competing demands,
    concrete observation, withheld action or claim, and reopening condition.
    Link the relevant existing pattern and version; open a candidate only
    when that pattern does not adequately express the observation. An
    unresolved candidate is legal and must not pretend to supply a solution.
    Name a proposal owner and the affected roles; offer those roles a place
    to challenge scope, evidence, and cost. Record the adoption authority
    (currently Joe alone for workshop-family rules),
    decision, reasons, and effective scope/version. Until that decision,
    candidate status grants no new enforcement or sanctioning authority.

  + BECAUSE:
    A refusal preserves attention when others can inspect what was
    noticed and what would permit reconsideration. Open capture and explicit
    adoption let the language learn without turning each author's proposal
    into an obligation for everybody else.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Use workshop/distinguishing-cases-before-adoption to test the proposed response.]
    next[Review an attempted promotion without a named decision; it must remain a candidate.]
    next[Record repeated refusals against the existing candidate rather than minting duplicates.]
